\title{Definitional Equalities for Substitutions using Rewrite Rules}

\author{Marius Weidner}
\email{weidner@cs.uni-freiburg.de}
\orcid{0009-0008-1152-165X}
\affiliation{%
  \institution{University of Freiburg}
  \city{Freiburg}
  \country{Germany}}

\keywords{Explicit Substitutions, Term Rewrite Systems, Agda}

\ccsdesc[300]{Theory of computation~Equational logic and rewriting}

\input{systemf}
\input{examples}

\begin{abstract}

  Choosing a representation of variable binding is a central problem in
  mechanizing programming language metatheory. De~Bruijn indices provide a
  canonical representation, but developments built on them require numerous
  lemmas about how substitutions compose and how they pass under binders, and
  every one of those lemmas has to be applied by hand or by a tactic.

  The $σ$-calculus makes parallel substitutions first-class and normalizes them
  by rewriting. Modeling substitutions as functions turns its rules into
  theorems, which libraries such as \textsc{Autosubst} exploit, but the resulting
  equalities stay propositional. We register the $σ$-calculus as rewrite rules in
  Agda, so that substitutions are normalized during conversion by the type
  checker rather than by a tactic.

  We first solve the issue that the rules are provable only if the operations
  reduce, and eligible for rewriting only if the same definitions are stuck. We
  then identify which $σ$-calculus variant meets both requirements, and derive a
  rewrite system where renamings stay first-class, because a proof assistant
  needs them to define substitution by structural recursion.

  A code generator emits the whole development from a syntax description. Every
  rule is registered together with a proof of the equality it installs, so
  logical consistency is preserved, and confluence of the resulting system keeps
  the type checker sound. To test the rules at scale we solve the POPLmark and
  POPLmark Reloaded challenges. We see this as groundwork for intrinsically typed
  formalizations of languages with a more complex type structure.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Handling variable binding remains a central challenge in mechanizing
programming language metatheory. Compared to pen-and-paper proofs, binders in
proof assistants are a notorious source of uninteresting technicalities and
boilerplate. Many representations have been proposed to contain them, among
them locally nameless binders~\cite{Chargueraud2012}, nominal
syntax~\cite{10.1007/11532231_4, Nominal2-AFP}, higher-order abstract
syntax~\cite{PfenningElliott1988}, and de~Bruijn indices~\cite{deBruijn1972}.
De~Bruijn syntax makes $α$-equivalent terms syntactically identical, and the
syntax itself is easy to define. A development built on de~Bruijn indices still
requires a long list of lemmas about substitution composition and about the
interaction of substitution with binders.

On paper, substitution usually replaces a single variable. In a proof assistant
one instead works with parallel substitutions, which map variables to terms and
replace all of them simultaneously. The $σ$-calculus~\cite{10.1145/96709.96712}
makes such maps first-class objects with primitive syntactic operations for
identity, weakening, extension and composition, together with a rewrite system
that normalizes expressions built from them. The de~Bruijn
algebra~\cite{10.1145/2676724.2693163} is the canonical semantic model for
those operations, a two-sorted algebra over de~Bruijn terms and substitutions,
with substitutions modeled as total functions from variables to terms. In that
model the rules are provable theorems, which is what makes them usable for
equational reasoning in proof assistants.
\textsc{Autosubst}~\cite{schafer2015autosubst} and its successor
\textsc{Autosubst 2}~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101} generate the primitive
operations, the proofs of the laws, and a tactic that normalizes substitutions
inside a goal from a syntax description.

The equalities obtained by \textsc{Autosubst} are propositional. Normalization
is left to the user, so every goal that mentions a substitution has to be
brought into normal form before it can be closed. In Rocq this is close to
automatic, because \textsc{Autosubst} supplies a tactic that performs the whole
normalization procedure, but what the tactic produces is still a propositional
equality. Recent support for user-defined rewrite rules in
Agda~\cite{cockx:LIPIcs.TYPES.2019.2, 10.1145/3434341} and
Rocq~\cite{leray_et_al:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.26} hands normalization to the type
checker. A proof of a propositional equality can be registered as a rule, and
the type checker then reduces its left-hand side to its right-hand side, so the
equality holds definitionally. The difference can be large for intrinsically
typed syntax, when substitutions appears in a type index and every use of a
propositional law becomes a transport~\cite{stronglytyped}.

Consistency of rewrite rules is retained as long as the registered rules are
supported by proofs and the resulting system is
confluent~\cite{10.1145/3434341}. Agda applies a rewrite rule by matching a
stuck term (i.e.\ an irreducable term that is headed by a defined function or a
constructor) against the left-hand side of the registered
proof~\cite{10.1007/978-3-642-21691-6_5}.

Whether the left-hand side of a rule from the $σ$-calculus can be matched by
type checker is decided by how the operations in it are defined. The same
choice decides whether that law is provable. Modeled as functions from
variables to terms or as vectors of terms, the $σ$-calculus operations reduce,
and every law is provable. Under that same reduction, the left-hand sides for
some rules are not stuck, and these laws cannot be registered for rewriting.

If we decide to model the operations as constructors, every left-hand side is
stuck by construction, but we cannot derive proofs for the laws.
Wadler~\cite{Wadler_2024} takes a minimal version of the constrcutor approach,
making only weakening a constructor in the syntax, thereby successfully
avoiding renamings. But weakening then does not push inwards into terms, and
the definitional equalities that would push it there are unrecoverable.

We choose a definition with an underlying model whose laws are provable, and
block their reduction artificially. Agda's opaque
definitions~\cite{gratzer2022controlling} give the required control over the
reduction behavior of definitions. A function declared opaque does not reduce
outside its block, and an unfolding instruction lets a later block see through
it again. Hence we can see the operations as ordinary functions where the
proofs need them and as stuck definitions where the rules need to match them.

Opacity settles how the operations are defined, and leaves open which
$σ$-calculus variant can be safely registered as rewrite rules. Registering
safely means that every rule comes with a proof to preserve logical
consistency, and that the rule set as a whole is confluent to keep the type
checker sound. To make the matter more complicated, we also want renamings to
be first class, because a proof assistant needs them to define substitution by
structural recursion.
\subsection*{Contributions}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{A $\sigma$-calculus.}
        We give an $σ$-calculus variant with first-class renamings, that we can make definitional in a
        multi-sorted, intrinsically scoped setting using Agda. Every rule is proved before it is
        registered, hence logical consistency is preserved.
  \item \textbf{A metatheoretic study of the rewrite system.}
        Agda's built-in checker can validate local confluence,
        and using an external checker we can prove termination and confluence of the
        first-order export, which keeps the type checker sound.
  \item \textbf{A code generator.}
        We supply a script that emits the entire infrastructure from a syntax description.
  \item \textbf{An evaluation of the approach.}
        We solve the POPLmark Challenge~\cite{poplmark2005}, Parts 1A, 1B, 2A and~2B, and POPLmark Reloaded~\cite{poplmarkreloaded2019},
        on definitions and the rewrite system generated by the script. Only one
        substitution fact remains propositional: a non-trivial substitution that is a renaming in disguise.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Structure}

In \cref{sec:pre} we explain the intriniscally scoped syntax setting we work in
and define renaming and substitution over it. In \cref{sec:mis} we state the
conditions Agda puts on a rewrite rule, identify the $σ$-calculus variant that
meets them, derive the rules that follow with first class renamings, and
establish confluence and termination. In \cref{sec:fwk} we exercise the system
on the metatheory of System~F, explain how to generate the infrastructure from
a syntax description, and run it at scale on the POPLmark challenge.
\Cref{sec:rwk} discusses related work, \cref{sec:future} collects future work,
and \cref{sec:con} concludes.

\section{Syntax and Substitution}\label{sec:pre}

This section only establishes the required definitions. The development is
carried out in Agda throughout. We give the multi-sorted and intriniscally
scoped syntax of the paper (\cref{sec:pre-syn}) and define renaming and
substitution over it (\cref{sec:mis-sub}). Two features of these definitions
are important for what \cref{sec:mis} can register as rewrite rules. Variables
form a sort of their own, generated by zero and successor, as is standard.
Renamings are defined separately, because substitution cannot be defined
structurally recursivly without them.

\subsection{Multi-Sorted and Scoped Syntax}\label{sec:pre-syn}
\begin{figure*}[t]
  \AgdaNoSpaceAroundCode{}
  \setlength{\mathindent}{0pt}
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.44\linewidth}
    \raggedright{}
    \EScoped{}
  \end{minipage}
  \hfill
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.52\linewidth}
    \raggedright{}
    \EMultiSorted{}
    \EMultiSortedTm{}
  \end{minipage}
  \caption{System~F as classically scoped syntax (left) and as the multi-sorted
    scoped syntax we use (right)}\label{fig:pre-svm}
\end{figure*}

Many programming languages feature several syntactic categories. For instance,
System~F~\cite{girard1972, 10.1007/3-540-06859-7_148} has three: expressions,
types, and kinds. Each usually gets its own datatype definition.
\Cref{fig:pre-svm} (left) shows the standard intrinsically scoped
implementation, where types are indexed by the number of type variables and
expressions by the numbers of type and expression variables. Variables are
correct-by-construction de~Bruijn indices of type \AgdaDatatype{Fin}
\AgdaBound{n}, the natural numbers smaller than~\AgdaBound{n}. Thus, scoped
syntax rules out scoping errors without committing to full intrinsic typing.

The standard representation forces one substitution operation per pair of
sorts, such as type-in-type, type-in-expression and expression-in-expression.
This is the representation \textsc{Autosubst 2} chooses, and it explains why
their rewrite system needs to be extended with vector substitutions, which
bundle the substitutions over the different sorts and come with normalization
rules of their own.

To avoid this combinatorial explosion we unify the syntax along two orthogonal
dimensions, shown in \cref{fig:pre-svm} (right). Variables and terms form a
single datatype \AgdaBound{S} \AgdaDatatype{⊢[} \AgdaBound{m} \AgdaDatatype{]}
\AgdaBound{s}, indexed by a \AgdaDatatype{Mode} \AgdaBound{m}, a target
\AgdaDatatype{Sort} \AgdaBound{s} and a \AgdaDatatype{Scope} \AgdaBound{S}, a
list of sorts. Variables of type \AgdaBound{S} \AgdaDatatype{∋} \AgdaBound{s}
are represented across all sorts at once, the length of \AgdaBound{S} being the
number of variables in scope and the position of \AgdaBound{s} in \AgdaBound{S}
the de~Bruijn index. Renamings and substitutions defined over this syntax then
operate uniformly across sorts, and vector substitutions become unnecessary.

Recent work extends it to the maps over that
syntax~\cite{altenkirch2025substitution, ren-sub}, deriving renaming and
substitution from a single traversal that abstracts over what a variable is
mapped to, a variable or a term. Our syntax admits the same treatment, and some
substitution laws would then follow uniformly for renamings and substitutions
rather than twice in similar shape. We keep renaming and substitution apart for
clarity of presentation, and because several of our rules treat the two
differently.

\subsection{Renaming and Substitution}\label{sec:mis-sub}

Instantiation, the operation that applies a substitution to a term, when
defined by mutual recursion with composition is not structurally recursive. As
is standard, we follow Adams~\cite{10.1007/11617990_1} and define instantiation
with renamings first. Renamings then run through the whole development, and
proofs about them need the same reasoning we want to automate for
substitutions. We will later follow \textsc{Autosubst 2} and put both into the
equational theory and relate the two, when possible.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \AgdaNoSpaceAroundCode{}
  \setlength{\mathindent}{0pt}
  \setlength{\AgdaEmptySkip}{0.55\baselineskip}
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.46\linewidth}
    \raggedright{}
    \ERen{}
    \vspace{0.2\baselineskip}
    \ERenOps{}
  \end{minipage}
  \hfill
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.50\linewidth}
    \raggedright{}
    \ESub{}
    \vspace{0.2\baselineskip}
    \ESubT{}
    \vspace{0.55\baselineskip}
    \begin{minipage}[t]{\linewidth}\raggedright{}\ESubIdWk{}\end{minipage}
  \end{minipage}
  \caption{The operations for renaming (left) and for substitution (right)}\label{fig:mis-ras}
\end{figure*}

We model renamings and substitutions as functions in \cref{fig:mis-ras}. A
renaming (\AgdaFunction{\_→ᴿ\_}) takes every variable to a variable of the same
sort, changing only the scope. A substitution (\AgdaFunction{\_→ˢ\_}) takes it
to a term of that sort instead. We call either of them a \emph{map} where the
distinction does not matter. A map then has a mode as well, the mode of its
images, \AgdaInductiveConstructor{V} for a renaming and
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{T} for a substitution. The operations over maps are
functions as well, and all of them are placed in an \AgdaKeyword{opaque} block.
They stay stuck, and we unfold them only in the proofs that need their
computational behavior.

Modeling maps as functions is a choice. A vector of variables or terms, one per
variable in the domain scope, would do as well, and would need the same
opacity. Extension then is a constructor and stuck by itself, but every other
operation is still a definition that unfolds. We choose the function model
because we find it easier to think about, and accept that in return we then
need to postulate function extensionality to work with equalities between maps.
We return to the vector model in \cref{sec:vec}.

The identity (\AgdaFunction{idᴿ}) takes every variable to itself. Weakening
(\AgdaFunction{wkᴿ}) takes a variable to its successor, and takes the sort that
is weakened by explicitly, because Agda frequently fails to infer it. Extension
(\AgdaFunction{\_∙ᴿ\_}) adds an image for a newly bound variable, and lifting
(\AgdaFunction{\_↑ᴿ\_}) fixes that variable and weakens the images of the rest,
which is what lets a renaming pass under a binder.

Instantiation (\AgdaFunction{\_[\_]ᴿ}) traverses a term and lifts whenever it
enters a binder. It is defined on variables as well, so that a lookup is
written \AgdaBound{x} \AgdaFunction{[} \AgdaBound{ξ} \AgdaFunction{]ᴿ} rather
than as the application \AgdaBound{ξ} \AgdaBound{\_} \AgdaBound{x}. We only
show three of its clauses, for a variable, a lambda binding and an application,
and omits the five that are evident congruences. Composition
(\AgdaFunction{\_⨟ᴿ\_}) looks a variable up in the first renaming and renames
the result with the second, hence it's only ordinary function composition in
disguise.

The coercion operator (\AgdaFunction{⟨\_⟩}) embeds a renaming in a
substitution, wrapping each image variable in the variable constructor. The
remaining substitution operations are the same one level up, with terms in
place of variables, and the figure lists them in that order. Extension
(\AgdaFunction{\_∙ˢ\_}) reads as before. Lifting (\AgdaFunction{\_↑ˢ\_})
weakens the images of the rest with the weakening renaming, which is what keeps
the recursion structural. Instantiation (\AgdaFunction{\_[\_]ˢ}) and
composition (\AgdaFunction{\_⨟ˢ\_}) read as before as well, except that a
lookup now returns a term. The identity (\AgdaFunction{idˢ}) and weakening
(\AgdaFunction{wkˢ}) substitutions are non-opaquely defined in terms of the
coercion operator.

\section{Rewrite Rules for Renamings and Substitutions}\label{sec:mis}

We now search for a $σ$-calculus variant that is eligible for rewriting. What
we require is a finite set of rules that is confluent on metavariables, and not
every variant provides one for the definitions we gave in the previous section.
The variants are stated for substitutions alone, so the rules for renamings
have to be worked out as well. We state the conditions Agda puts on a rewrite
rule in \cref{sec:pre-agd}, identify a variant that meets them in
\cref{sec:pre-sig}, derive the rewrite system that follows with first class
renamings in \cref{sec:mis-sig}, and establish confluence and termination in
\cref{sec:ext}. Throughout, the multi-sorted scoped System~F is the running
example, but the construction applies to any multi-sorted scoped syntax.

\subsection{Rewrite Rules in Agda}\label{sec:pre-agd}

User-defined rewrite rules~\cite{10.1145/3434341, cockx:LIPIcs.TYPES.2019.2}
extend Agda's normalization-by-evaluation approach for definitional equalities
by treating propositional equalities as reduction rules. They can be seen as a
middle ground between intensional and extensional type theory, where type
checking stays decidable as long as the rules are confluent and
terminating~\cite{10.1145/3434341}.

An equality proof of the shape $\AgdaFunction{f}\, p_1 \ldots p_n \
  \AgdaFunction{≡}\ v$ is eligible for rewriting under three conditions.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \AgdaFunction{f} is a postulate, a defined function or a constructor.
  \item The left-hand side is stuck on \AgdaFunction{f}, that is, the application does
        not reduce.
  \item Every variable bound by the rule occurs on the left-hand side, and occurs there
        in pattern position.
\end{itemize}

The last condition serves matching. A bound variable appears in pattern
position if it is either not applied at all, or applied to distinct bound
variables and to nothing else. Applying a rule means matching its left-hand
side against a stuck term, and that match has to determine every variable the
rule binds. Matching is higher-order~\cite{10.1007/978-3-642-21691-6_5}, that
is, a variable bound by the rule may stand for a function and occur applied on
the left-hand side. In general that problem is undecidable, and the restriction
to pattern position makes it decidable.

For example, renaming a term by the identity leaves it unchanged. The law is
proved by induction on the term, so it does not hold definitionally on its own:
\ERewrite{} It meets the three conditions: the left-hand side is stuck on the
defined function \AgdaFunction{\_[\_]ᴿ}, because no clause matches a term using
a variable pattern, and its only bound variable \AgdaBound{t} occurs there
unapplied. Once it is registered via \ERewriteIt{}, Agda treats \AgdaBound{t}
\AgdaFunction{[} \AgdaFunction{idᴿ} \AgdaFunction{]ᴿ} as definitionally equal
to \AgdaBound{t}: \ERewriteEx{}

Beyond the local restrictions, the global set of rewritten equations have to be
well behaved. We want to preserve two guarantees of the proof assistant. The
type checker is sound only if conversion preserves types, and this needs
confluence of all registered rewrite rules with respect to the already existing
reductions. Logical consistency is preserved when all rules are instantiated
with propositional equality proofs~\cite[Sect.~3 and 6.4]{10.1145/3434341}

We prove all rules before registering them. Confluence is decided on the
critical pairs of the rule set. The terms to which two reductions/rewrites
apply, need to reduce to a common term. A rule has to be joinable against every
rule other rule, and against the reduction Agda performs on its own, hence a
rule interacts with the defining clauses of every function it mentions, which
is why we keep the $σ$-calculus operations opaque.

\subsection{Choosing the Correct Variant}\label{sec:pre-sig}
%TODO(structure, later sections): leave the next subsubsection as is.  Then a
% final subsection on the registration in Agda, containing the termination part
% and the local confluence.  "Why the Definitions Must Not Unfold" is obsolete
% and so is the vector discussion, both move to the implementation section.

The $σ$-calculus exists in several variants, and they differ on properties the
registration as rewrite rules depends on. A $λσ$-calculus additionally includes
the $β$ and other reduction rules of the object language, which in our setting
belong to the user's language description and lie outside the theory we
register. Our rules deal only with substitutions. We forget about renamings for
now, so for the purpose of this subsection \ag{idˢ} and \ag{wkˢ} are constants
of the calculus.

\begin{figure*}[!t]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzcd}[column sep=small, row sep=normal]
    & (\agc{zero}\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{∙ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ})
    \arrow[dl, "η"'] \arrow[dr, "\text{weakening}"]                                    & \\
    \ag{wkˢ} & &
    (\agc{`}\,(\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero})) \agop{∙ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ})
    \arrow[ll, dashed, "\text{add } ρ_1"]
    \arrow[dl, dotted, "\text{wrapped in } \_ \agop{⨟ˢ} σ"]                            \\
    & ((\agc{`}\,(\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero})) \agop{∙ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ}))
    \agop{⨟ˢ} σ
    \arrow[dl, "ρ_1"'] \arrow[dr, "\text{distributivity} + \text{assoc}"]                      & \\
    \ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} σ & &
    ((\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{∙ˢ}
    (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} σ))
    \arrow[ll, dashed, "\text{add } ρ_2"] \arrow[dl, dotted, "σ := \ag{wkˢ}"]          \\
    & ((\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero})\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{∙ˢ}
    (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ}))
    \arrow[dl, "ρ_2"'] \arrow[dr, "\text{weakening}"]                                  & \\
    \ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ} & &
    (\agc{`}\,(\agc{suc}\,(\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero}))) \agop{∙ˢ}
    (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ}))
    \arrow[ll, dashed, "\text{add } ρ_3"]
    \arrow[dl, dotted, "\text{wrapped in } \_ \agop{⨟ˢ} σ"]                            \\
    & \vdots &                                                                         \\
  \end{tikzcd}
  \caption{Attempt to complete the $η$-law against inductive variables}\label{fig:eta-completion}
\end{figure*}

\textbf{The original $\sigma$-calculus}. All variants share a set of rules that goes back to the original calculus of
Abadi et al.~\cite{10.1145/96709.96712}, where lifting is defined in terms of
the other operators: $σ \agop{↑ˢ} s = (\agc{`}\,\agc{zero}) \agop{∙ˢ} (σ
  \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ}\,s)$. We group them into four categories and name them after
Stark et al.~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101}.
\begin{itemize}
  \item Reduction laws push an instantiation through term constructors. There is one
        for every constructor of the syntax, as in
        \[
          (\agc{λx}\,e)\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} = \agc{λx}\,(e\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{↑ˢ}
          s\,\ag{]ˢ})
        \] They are exactly the clauses of the instantiation operator of
        \cref{sec:mis-sub}.
  \item The defining clauses of the operations evaluate a substitution at a variable,
        so they fix the meaning of extension, weakening and lifting, for example \[
          \agc{zero}\,\ag{[}\,t
          \agop{∙ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ} = t
        \]
  \item Interference laws govern the interplay of composition, extension and weakening,
        which include associativity of composition and distributivity of composition
        over extension:
        \begin{align*}
          (σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2) \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_3 & = σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} (σ_2 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_3)                       \\
          (t \agop{∙ˢ} σ_1) \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2   & = (t\,\ag{[}\,σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{∙ˢ} (σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2)
        \end{align*}
  \item The monad laws relate instantiation to composition. The identity substitute
        leaves a term untouched and compositionality fuses two instantiations into one:
        \begin{align*}
          t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}                    & = t                        \\
          (t\,\ag{[}\,σ_1\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ} & = t\,\ag{[}\,σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ}
          σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}
        \end{align*}
\end{itemize}

In our model, where substitutions are functions, the reduction laws and the
defining clauses of the operations hold by computation. The monad laws are
proved by induction on the term and the interference laws are proved using
function extensionality.

The original $σ$-calculus establishes confluence on ground terms only. That
does not suffice for a rewrite system inside a proof assistant, because a rule
applies wherever the type checker finds a match and the terms it encounters
contain metavariables.

\textbf{The $\sigma_{SP}$-calculus}.
Curien et al.~\cite{10.1145/226643.226675} repair this by adding new rules.
Schäfer et al.~\cite{10.1145/2676724.2693163} later show that the calculus is
complete for the model in which substitutions are functions from variables to
terms, so every equation that holds is derivable from the rules, and
Stark~\cite{Stark:2020:Mechanising} tabulates them. It is the version used in
\textsc{Autosubst} and thus the obvious candidate for this work. Unfortunately
it clashes with our inductive definition of variables.

The new rules are the two $η$-laws. The general one equates a substitution with
its value at the first variable extended by its restriction to the remaining
ones, $(\agc{zero}\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{∙ˢ} (\ag{wkˢ}\,s \agop{⨟ˢ} σ) =
  σ$, and its instance at the identity gives $(\agc{`}\,\agc{zero}) \agop{∙ˢ}
  \ag{wkˢ}\,s = \ag{idˢ}$. They can be seen as surjective pairing for
substitutions.

The left-hand side of $η$ mentions $σ$ twice, under a lookup and under a
composition, so it is not left-linear. Every rule that reduces one of them and
not the other opens a critical pair. Inductive variables force the law
$x\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkˢ}\,s\,\ag{]ˢ} = \agc{`}\,(\agc{suc}\,x)$, because with the
variables generated by $\agc{zero}$ and $\agc{suc}$, the weakened variable and
$\agc{suc}\,x$ are two terms for the same variable, and pattern matching on
variables needs the constructor form to be the normal form.

Now instantiate $σ$ in $η$ with a weakening, which opens the pair in the top
row of \cref{fig:eta-completion}. No rule collapses the extension on its right,
because the only rules matching an extension are the $η$-laws and both need the
head to be $\agc{zero}$. Joining it means adding the rule $ρ_1$ of the figure,
and that rule has an extension on its left-hand side. Distributivity fires on
an extension that is the left operand of a composition, so $ρ_1$ and
distributivity overlap and the middle row is their pair. The rule $ρ_2$ that
joins that one carries a lookup in the head, so instantiating $σ$ with a
weakening evaluates the lookup and reaches the bottom row, which is the top row
with one more $\agc{suc}$.

\begin{observation}\label{obs:eta-inductive}
  Inductive variables and the $η$-laws cannot coexist. Together they leave
  non-joinable critical pairs that no finite completion closes.
\end{observation}

$σ_{SP}$ has no $\agc{suc}$. The $n$-th variable is represented as
$\agc{zero}$ under $n$ weakenings, so a lookup at a weakening is already a
normal form, and with the operations declared as \AgdaKeyword{postulate}s its
equations together with the $η$-laws pass the confluence checker. If we wish to
eliminate the postulates to guarantee consistency and usability inside proofs,
our only choice is weakening inside the term syntax, which is the work
of Wadler on explicit weakenings. But then weakening does not push into terms, and
we could not instantiate all the $σ$-calculus laws we require with proofs. The
reduction law for application, $(t_1 \agop{·} t_2)\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} =
  (t_1\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}) \agop{·} (t_2\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ})$, is one of
them.

\textsc{Autosubst} can combine inductive
variables with the $η$-laws because a tactic has more freedom than a rewrite
rule. It chooses where and in which order to apply an equation. Confluence on metavariables
is not a critical property for tactics.

Losing the $η$-laws is severe when lifting is defined in terms of the other
operators, as it is in $σ_{SP}$. Lifting the identity is then the extension
$(\agc{`}\,\agc{zero}) \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{wkˢ}\,s$, and the $η$-law at the identity
is the only rule that collapses it back to $\ag{idˢ}$. A lifted identity
appears whenever a substitution passes under a binder, so we would be stuck
frequently. Without the $η$-laws $σ_{SP}$ therefore has nothing left to offer
us, and we instead look at a variant that makes lifting a primitive operation.

\textbf{The $\sigma_\Uparrow$-calculus}.
This version comes from the same paper by Curien et
al.~\cite{10.1145/226643.226675} and is confluent on open terms. Its variables are generated inductively,
and a weakened variable reduces to its successor, which is what we require.
The drawback is that the $η$-laws hold only propositionally, so the calculus
is not complete. We forfeit completeness in any case because we need renamings
as a second kind of map, and the transfer between renamings and substitutions
is incomplete.

A later paper by Hardin et al.~\cite{HMP98} tabulates the 23 rules of
$σ_\Uparrow$, which fall into the four categories above together with a family
of rules under a composition. Lifting is an operator of the calculus and
requires laws of its own, for example $\ag{idˢ} \agop{↑ˢ} s = \ag{idˢ}$ and
$(σ_1 \agop{↑ˢ} s) \agop{⨟ˢ} (σ_2 \agop{↑ˢ} s) = (σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2) \agop{↑ˢ}
  s$. The first is what the $η$-law had to supply where lifting is defined, and
the second collapses nested binders.

None of these variants provides renamings, which are required for termination
in proof assistants. The standard repair is to define renamings
first~\cite{10.1007/11617990_1}. \textsc{Autosubst~2} takes the further step of
making renamings first-class and extending the equational theory with rules
that relate renamings and substitutions~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101}. Their
extended system also is no longer complete, and its confluence was not formally
verified~\cite{Stark:2020:Mechanising}.

\subsection{Extension with Renamings}\label{sec:mis-sig}
\begin{figure*}[!t]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzcd}[column sep=tiny, row sep=normal]
    & (x\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}
    \arrow[dl, "\text{weakening}"'] \arrow[dr, "\text{fold}"]                 & \\
    \agc{suc}\,(x\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}) & &
    x\,\ag{[}\,ξ \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}
    \arrow[ll, dashed, "\text{add } κ_1"]
    \arrow[dl, dotted, "ξ := ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2"]                              \\
    & x\,\ag{[}\,(ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2) \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}
    \arrow[dl, "κ_1"'] \arrow[dr, "\text{assoc}"]                             & \\
    \agc{suc}\,(x\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}) & &
    x\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} (ξ_2 \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s)\,\ag{]ᴿ}
    \arrow[ll, dashed, "\text{add } κ_2"]
    \arrow[dl, dotted, "ξ_2 := ξ_2 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_3"]                            \\
    & \vdots &                                                                 \\
  \end{tikzcd}
  \caption{Attempt to complete the fold for renamings against weakening}\label{fig:push-forced}
\end{figure*}

The extended rule set includes a copy of $σ_\Uparrow$ for the maps of each
mode, with the reduction laws instantiated to the constructors of the
signature. Additional rules connect the two modes. This section only presents
the connection and the rules where renamings and substitutions need to be
treated differently.

Renamings and substitutions are connected using the coercion operator
$\ag{⟨\_⟩}$. Lifting a substitution weakens the images of the rest with the
weakening renaming, which is what keeps the recursion structural, and \ag{idˢ}
and \ag{wkˢ} are the coerced renamings $\ag{⟨}\,\ag{idᴿ}\,\ag{⟩}$ and
$\ag{⟨}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{⟩}$. Due to that we need to make sure substitutions
that are coerced renamings reduce to actual renamings for the appropiate rules
to apply. For instance, the $\mathsf{coincidence}$
law~\cite{Stark:2020:Mechanising} is oriented that way,
\[
  t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩}\,\ag{]ˢ} = t\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}
\]
A term instantiated with an embedded renaming is renamed directly. The
remaining coercion rules keep embedded renamings closed under the substitution
operations by moving $\ag{⟨\_⟩}$ outward, as in $\ag{⟨}\,ξ_1\,\ag{⟩} \agop{⨟ˢ}
  \ag{⟨}\,ξ_2\,\ag{⟩} = \ag{⟨}\,ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2\,\ag{⟩}$, which we call
$\ag{⟨\_⟩}\text{-}\mathsf{comp}$, and $(\ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩} \agop{↑ˢ} s) =
  \ag{⟨}\,ξ \agop{↑ᴿ} s\,\ag{⟩}$.

Because variables and terms are separate sorts, compositionality splits into
eight laws where the $σ$-calculus has one, the four combinations of the two
modes, each at terms and at variables. Each is registered in one of two
directions. We call the direction that merges two instantiations into one
instantiation under a composed map a fold, and the direction that takes a
composed map apart into two applications a push. The eight compositionality
rules point as follows:
\begin{align*}
  (t\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}   & = t\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}             & \text{fold} \\
  x\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1 \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}             & = (x\,\ag{[}\,ξ_1\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}   & \text{push} \\
  (t\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}       & = t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩} \agop{⨟ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ} & \text{fold} \\
  x\,\ag{[}\,\ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩} \agop{⨟ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ} & = (x\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}       & \text{push} \\
  (t\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}       & = t\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩}\,\ag{]ˢ} & \text{fold} \\
  (x\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}       & = x\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩}\,\ag{]ˢ} & \text{fold} \\
  (t\,\ag{[}\,σ_1\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}   & = t\,\ag{[}\,σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}             & \text{fold} \\
  (x\,\ag{[}\,σ_1\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}   & = x\,\ag{[}\,σ_1 \agop{⨟ˢ} σ_2\,\ag{]ˢ}             & \text{fold}
\end{align*}
The direction follows what the first application produces. A renaming applied
to a variable yields a variable, and there we push. Where the first map is a
substitution the two sorts agree and the development registers them as a single
mode-generic rule.

% The direction matters because
% a rule fires one way only. A fold makes the single instantiation the normal
% form, a push makes the staged applications the normal form, and for variables
% the choice is forced due to inductive variables. For example, weakening
% followed by a renaming fuses into a single instantiation applied to a term,
% $(t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ} = t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s
%   \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}$. The lookup of a variable under the same composed
% map need to run in stages, after which the defining clause of weakening can
% continue, $\agc{zero}\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ} =
%   (\agc{zero}\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ} =
%   (\agc{suc}\,\agc{zero})\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ}$.

At variables the push is forced. Because weakening needs to be related to the
successor, $(x\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}$ reduces to
$\agc{suc}\,(x\,\ag{[}\,ξ\,\ag{]ᴿ})$. Under a fold at variables it also reduces
to $x\,\ag{[}\,ξ \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}$, and the only sound join is a
rule matching $ξ \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s$, a composition whose interesting part
is the right operand, which conflict the right associativity.
\Cref{fig:push-forced} follows the attempt to complete the fold, where
associativity moves the weakening one composition deeper at every step. Note
that left associativity would have a similar issue.

\begin{observation}\label{obs:fold-push}
  Compositionality laws for variables with renamings on the left are registered as push, instead of a fold.
\end{observation}

The push is mirroring the definition of composition. This is not a coincidence,
because we want to apply the definition for composition, exactly when we can
later apply a defining law for renamings to reduce it to an inductive variable.

\subsection{The Full Rewrite System}
The rest of the rules deal with the completion of rewrite system that resulted
from our choices in the last two sections.


\Cref{tab:groups} counts the resulting system by group.
Only the reduction laws depend on the
signature. We use $|c|$ to denote the amount of constructors the syntax has.
Every other group is the same for any syntax. The 4 groups above the middle
line are the four categories of \cref{sec:pre-sig}, instantiated for the maps of
each mode. The
push and coercion rules below the line have no counterpart in
$σ_\Uparrow$.

\begin{table}[b]
  \centering\footnotesize
  \caption{The registered rules by group}\label{tab:groups}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}rlp{0.52\linewidth}@{}}
    \toprule
    \textbf{\#}  & \textbf{group}         & \textbf{content}                                                                                    \\
    \midrule
    $2*|c|$      & reduction laws         & push an instantiation through a constructor, one per constructor and mode                       \\
    11           & defining clauses       & evaluate a map at a variable, fixing extension, weakening and lifting                       \\
    23           & interference laws      & the interplay of composition, extension, weakening and lifting, in both modes \\
    10           & monad laws             & identity and compositionality, in all four mode combinations                                        \\
    \midrule
    \phantom{0}6 & push rules             & completion rules for the push strategy                                                              \\
    \phantom{0}9 & coercion               & rules that collapse coercions into renamings                                                        \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

Associativity nests compositions to the right. A rule whose left-hand side is
itself a composition can then stop matching, because composing it with a
further map moves that map into its right operand. The interference law 
interact, $\ag{wkᴿ}\,s \agop{⨟ᴿ} (x \agop{∙ᴿ} ξ) = ξ$, shows the
effect. In $(\ag{wkᴿ}\,s \agop{⨟ᴿ} (x \agop{∙ᴿ} ξ)) \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ′$ associativity
produces $\ag{wkᴿ}\,s \agop{⨟ᴿ} ((x \agop{∙ᴿ} ξ) \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ′)$, but
interact requires an extension on the outside.

$σ_\Uparrow$ repairs the match with distributivity, which rewrites the inner
composition back into an extension so that interact applies again.
We cannot follow it, because distributivity conflicts with the push rule.
The term $x\,\ag{[}\,(y \agop{∙ᴿ} ξ_1) \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}$
reduces by push to $(x\,\ag{[}\,y \agop{∙ᴿ} ξ_1\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}$
and by distributivity to $x\,\ag{[}\,(y\,\ag{[}\,ξ_2\,\ag{]ᴿ}) \agop{∙ᴿ} (ξ_1
  \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ_2)\,\ag{]ᴿ}$. Neither reduces further while $x$ is unknown, so
the pair does not join. The law for lifting, $(ξ \agop{↑ᴿ} s)
  \agop{⨟ᴿ} (x \agop{∙ᴿ} ξ′) = x \agop{∙ᴿ} (ξ \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ′)$, conflicts the
push strategy in the same way.

Instead we register the composed form directly,
\[
  \ag{wkᴿ}\,s \agop{⨟ᴿ} ((x \agop{∙ᴿ} ξ) \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ′) = ξ \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ′,
\]
which reaches the result of interact without passing through an
extension. One level of nesting is enough, because associativity returns any
deeper nesting to this shape.

The rule inherits its weakening from interact and is exactly as
general. Whether a rule needs such a companion depends on its right operand. In
$\ag{idᴿ} \agop{⨟ᴿ} ξ = ξ$ that operand is a map variable, so re-association
hands the rule a larger map and it still matches. In interact it is
an extension, which re-association buries under a composition. Three rules of
the renaming world need a companion for this reason, interact
together with the law that cancels a weakening against a lift and the law that
distributes a lift over a composition. We orient associativity as a rule, so
the three come out of ordinary completion. They have the shape of the extended
rules that completion modulo an associative-commutative operator
adds~\cite{PetersonStickel1981}.

The full rule set is available in the supplement. Because we have modeled maps
as functions, all rules can be proven propositionally (assuming function
extensionality) and then registered as rewrite rule.

\subsection{Correctness}\label{sec:ext}
What remains is confluence. To this end, we rely on Agda's opt-in
\texttt{--local-confluence-check}, which computes the critical pairs of the
rewrite system. It checks that all critical pairs can be joined using other
rewrite rules and internal reductions.

Agda also offers a global \texttt{--confluence-check}, which our system does
not pass. Whenever two left-hand sides overlap, their most general unifier has
to be a registered left-hand side as well. When the rule set includes
associativity this will always fail, because associativity overlaps itself at a
fourfold composition, and registering that instance only pushes the overlap to
a longer one. No finite rule set with an associative composition meets the
condition.

Agda does not check termination for rewrite rules. Under the global check
termination is \emph{not} a critical property~\cite{10.1145/3434341}. Local
confluence is the weaker property, and to conclude confluence using Newman's
lemma~\cite{Newman1942}, we need termination of the rewrite system. Proving
termination has to leave Agda and we export the rewrite system as a first-order
term rewriting system, with scope indices erased, and hand the resulting rules
to an external prover. AProVE~\cite{AProVE2017} successfully certifies
termination and confluence. The exported rule set and the termination
certificate can be found in the supplement.

Both Agda's local confluence check and the external termination check certify
the rule set as it is seen from outside the \AgdaKeyword{opaque} blocks, where
the operations do not reduce. We therefore carry an invariant that the type
checker cannot enforce.
\begin{quote}\label{inv:no-unfolding}
  Once a rewrite rule is registered, the opaque operations it mentions must
  never be unfolded again.
\end{quote}
We unfold the definitions only to prove the rules, and we prove all of them before
we register any of them as rewrites.

Together the two properties, the instantiation of the rules with propositional
equalities and confluence, preserve logical consistency and subject reduction.
Consistency makes the proofs meaningful and subject reduction keeps the type
checker sound.

\section{Implementation and Evaluation}\label{sec:fwk}
We first exercise the rule set on the metatheory of System~F
(\cref{sec:mis-ex-proof}). The rest of this section focuses on generalizing the
method. We generate the infrastructure from a syntax description
(\cref{sec:gen}) and measure on the POPLmark Challenge how much substitution
reasoning the rule set leaves (\cref{sec:eval}).

\subsection{Metatheory for System F}\label{sec:mis-ex-proof}

We show the rule set at work on a subject reduction proof for the System~F
version of \cref{fig:pre-svm}. Subject reduction rests on the
substitution-preserves-typing lemma, that applying a well typed substitution to
a well typed term preserves typing. That lemma rests in turn on the same
statement for renamings. We look at two occurences where normally substitution
reasoning is required, one from a typing rule and one from a reduction rule.
%And one obligation for renamings in the case of lifting.
The full development can also be found in the supplement.

The first comes from the typing rule for type application. Instantiating a
polymorphic type substitutes for the variable it binds, so the rule concludes
at the single substitution $t'\,\ag{[}\,t\,\ag{]₀}$, our notation for
$t'\,\ag{[}\,t \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}$. \ETyTApp{} In the
substitution-preserves-typing lemma this conclusion has to survive an outer
$σ$. The goal is $(t'\,\ag{[}\,t\,\ag{]₀})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}$, because that
is what the rule concludes with $σ$ applied to it. The sub-derivations instead
supply $(t'\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{↑ˢ}
  s\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,t\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}\,\ag{]₀}$, because the body was
typed under a lifted $σ$ and the argument under $σ$ alone. The two
substitutions have to agree, which is the lemma we call \ag{subst-commute}.

The second comes from call-by-value $β$-reduction. \EBetaLam{} The typing rule
for abstraction stores the result type of a function under \ag{weaken}, the
instantiation with the weakening renaming, $\ag{weaken}\ t =
  t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ} \,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}$, because the body is typed in a longer
context. \ETyLam{} That weakening is bookkeeping. Because scopes are shared
over sorts, binding an expression variable also extends the scope that types
are indexed by. Subject reduction for this redex therefore delivers the type
$(\ag{weaken}\ t_2)\,\ag{[}\,e_2\,\ag{]₀}$, while the goal is the plain $t_2$
the application concluded at. The weakening and the substitution have to
cancel, which is \ag{wk-cancel}.

Both are lemmas a de~Bruijn development needs to state, prove and and apply by
hand. Here both hold by \AgdaInductiveConstructor{refl}. \ELawsUsed{} For
\ag{wk-cancel} the weakening is a renaming and the single substitution is an
extension, so three rules suffice. {\small\begin{align*}
        & \rlap{$(\ag{weaken}\ t)\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{]₀} \;=\; (t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ})\,\ag{[}\,t' \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}$}                                          \\
  \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} (t' \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ})\,\ag{]ˢ}                                                                 &  & \text{\scriptsize compositionality} \\
  \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,\ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}                                                                                                   &  & \text{\scriptsize interact}         \\
  \to\; & t                                                                                                                              &  & \text{\scriptsize identity}
\end{align*}}%
The middle step is the interference law that cancels a weakening against an
extension. For \ag{subst-commute} the two sides start apart and meet in the
middle. {\small\begin{align*}
        & \rlap{$(t\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{↑ˢ} s\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}\,\ag{]₀}
              \;=\; (t\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{↑ˢ} s\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}$}                                          \\
  \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,(σ \agop{↑ˢ} s) \agop{⨟ˢ} (t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ})\,\ag{]ˢ}                   &  & \text{\scriptsize compositionality} \\
  \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} (σ \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{idˢ})\,\ag{]ˢ}                                 &  & \text{\scriptsize lift-cons}        \\
  \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ}                                                      &  & \text{\scriptsize comp-idᵣ}         \\[3pt]
        & \rlap{$(t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{]₀})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}
              \;=\; (t\,\ag{[}\,t' \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ}$}                                                                          \\
  \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,(t' \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}) \agop{⨟ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ}                                                     &  & \text{\scriptsize compositionality} \\
  \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} (\ag{idˢ} \agop{⨟ˢ} σ)\,\ag{]ˢ}                                 &  & \text{\scriptsize distributivity}   \\
  \to\; & t\,\ag{[}\,t'\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ} \agop{∙ˢ} σ\,\ag{]ˢ}                                                      &  & \text{\scriptsize comp-idₗ}
\end{align*}}%
Three of the laws in them are interference
laws we have not named so far.
\ag{lift-cons} composes a lifted map with an extension, where the bound
variable takes the extended term and everything above it passes to the tail.
\ag{comp-idₗ} and \ag{comp-idᵣ} are the two unit laws of composition.

% The third obligation involves renamings. Lifting a well typed substitution
% under a binder has to type the image of every variable in an extended context.
% In the induction case $\agc{suc}\,x$ a recursive call would not satisfy the
% termination checker, so \AgdaFunction{⊢↑ˢ} appeals to the lemma that renaming
% preserves typing, applied to the weakening renaming.
% 
% Lifting describes the image as
% $(\agc{suc}\,x)\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{↑ˢ} s\,\ag{]ˢ}$, while renaming-preserves-typing 
% delivers it as $(x\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}$. One
% rule on each side takes them to the same normal form, so the clause type checks
% with nothing supplied by hand. {\small\begin{align*}
%       & (\agc{suc}\,x)\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{↑ˢ} s\,\ag{]ˢ}                       \\
% \to\;   & x\,\ag{[}\,σ \agop{⨟ˢ} \ag{wkˢ}\,s\,\ag{]ˢ}
%         \quad\text{\scriptsize defining clause of lifting}                \\
% \gets\; & (x\,\ag{[}\,σ\,\ag{]ˢ})\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}
%         \quad\text{\scriptsize mixed compositionality}
% \end{align*}}%
% The second is the law that turns a substitution followed by a renaming into a
% single substitution, and it is only statable because renamings are maps in
% their own right.
% 
% Renaming preserves typing takes a well typed renaming as its second argument,
% so the call above has to supply one for the weakening renaming. That witness,
% \AgdaFunction{⊢wkᴿ}, is where \cref{obs:fold-push} is spent. It holds by
% \AgdaInductiveConstructor{refl} alone, because
% $x\,\ag{[}\,\ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}$ reduces to $\agc{suc}\,x$ and a context
% lookup at $\agc{suc}\,x$ is by definition the lookup at $x$. A fold at a
% variable would instead leave $x\,\ag{[}\,ξ \agop{⨟ᴿ} \ag{wkᴿ}\,s\,\ag{]ᴿ}$
% standing, the defining law for weakening would never fire, and
% \AgdaFunction{⊢wkᴿ} would need a lemma of its own.
% 
\subsection{Code Generation}\label{sec:gen}
\subsection*{\emph{The code generation script from this section was produced with help from  GenAI.}}

\begin{figure*}[!t]
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\linewidth}
    \begin{verbatim}
% open import Data.String using (String)
kind : Type
type : Type
expr : Type
row  : Type
pat  : Type

-- the constructors for kind
star : kind

-- the constructors for type
arr : type -> type -> type
all : kind -> (type -> type) -> type
rec : row -> type
    \end{verbatim}
  \end{minipage}
  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\linewidth}
    \begin{verbatim}
-- rows of labelled fields
rnil  : row
rcons : "String" -> type -> row -> row

-- the constructors for expr
app   : expr -> expr -> expr
lam   : type -> (expr -> expr) -> expr
tapp  : expr -> type -> expr
tlam  : kind -> (type -> expr) -> expr
proj  : expr -> "String" -> expr
match : #n -> expr -> pat -> (expr ^ n -> expr) -> expr

-- the constructors for pat
pvar  : pat
ppair : pat -> pat -> pat
    \end{verbatim}
  \end{minipage}
  \caption{System~F with record types and pattern
    matching}\label{fig:rel-hoa}
\end{figure*}

Nothing in \cref{sec:mis} is specific to System~F. Of the seven groups in
\cref{tab:groups}, only the reduction laws depend on the signature, and they
can be derived generically. We provide a script that turns a higher-order
abstract syntax (HOAS) description~\cite{schafer2015autosubst} into Agda code
with the rules ready for rewriting.

\Cref{fig:rel-hoa} shows such a description, our running example extended
with record types and pattern matching. It is the entire input needed to full rule set.
A line beginning with \texttt{\%} is passed through to
the preamble of the generated module. Binders are function arguments, that specifc the sort of the argument. Record labels are represented as strings. In general, external arguments can be
written in quotes, and the generated instantiations carry them along without
touching them. A pattern binds a variable number of expression variables at
once. The \texttt{match} constructor takes the arity \texttt{\#n} as its first argument and
then binds that many variables, which is what the iterated binder
\texttt{expr \^{} n} means. \textsc{Autosubst 2} does the same for
Rocq, emitting the scoped syntax together with the substitution lemmas that
need induction over it.

Several of \textsc{Autosubst 2}'s features come for free. Vector substitutions
are unnecessary and mutually recursive definitions work unchanged, both because
the syntax is multi-sorted. We do not support inline Agda code that depends on
the syntax, such as lists or products of terms. We note that they can be
encoded with additional sorts, at the cost of standard library support.

Variadic syntax is supported, i.e.\ a binder that binds a variable number of
variables at once, such as \texttt{match} does. It needs a new lifting
operation and a set of laws that supports it. The code generation includes a
new lifting operator $\ag{\_↑*[\_]\_}$, that takes a sort and a count and
iterates the ordinary lifting that many times. Additional laws support the new
operator.

The script is a single Python file with strict type checking enabled. It
includes a parser for HOAS descriptions and ad-hoc code generation, and it
carries all boilerplate Agda code so that others can run it standalone. Beside
the five languages of \cref{sec:eval} we generated a dozen files from the HOAS
descriptions shipped with \textsc{Autosubst 2}.

Although the model we use for maps is not interesting theoretically, it matters
for performance. The script generates the whole rewrite infrastructure for maps
modeled either as vectors or as functions.

\subsection{Vectors vs. Functions}\label{sec:vec}

A vector-like data type for maps is the other candidate representation that we
have not shown in this paper. Extension is a constructor, and identity,
weakening, lifting and composition stay defined by recursion over the vector,
so their clauses compete with the rules with respect to confluence exactly as
the function model's do, and opacity is also needed to block their reduction.
With vectors nothing is lost in terms of provability. Equality of vectors is
equality of data, so that variant needs no function extensionality postulate.

The vector model also type checks faster. We measured the time on the F$_{<:}$
signature with records and pattern matching, together with a large proof for
transitivity of subtyping with narrowing and preservation and progress on top
of it. The proof is 1{,}161 lines and is unchanged between the two models.
Together definitions and proofs take about 23\,s to type check with vectors
against about 145\,s with functions, a speedup of about six. Both figures were
taken on a standard developer machine.

We can only guess why the speedup is so large. One possibility is that Agda
optimizes matching against constructors, which here would only benefit the
extension constructor. Another is that the higher-order nature of maps as
functions leaks into the higher-order pattern unification that Agda performs to
decide whether a rule matches a stuck term. That should not happen, because the
operations we use are opaque, but maybe Agda still sees that the maps
underneath are defined as functions.

The proof we measured on is part of the POPLmark challenge.

\subsection{POPLmark and POPLmark Reloaded}\label{sec:eval}
\subsection*{\emph{The proofs from this section were produced with help from GenAI.}}


To test the construction at a scale where substitution reasoning hurts, we
solve the POPLmark Challenge~\cite{poplmark2005} and POPLmark
Reloaded~\cite{poplmarkreloaded2019} including its sums extension. The
development is roughly 8,400 lines of Agda, 6,100 of them excluding blanks and
comments, of which 2,800 are generated infrastructure and 3,200 metatheory.
\Cref{tab:coverage} lists the languages it involves and what is proved for
each.

\begin{table}[t]
  \caption{Coverage per language}\label{tab:coverage}
  \footnotesize
  \begin{tabular}{@{}llrr@{}}
    \toprule
    Language                    & POPLmark        & Generated LOC & Proof LOC \\
    \midrule
    STLC, sums                  & Reloaded 1a--2b & 504           & 728       \\
    F$_{<:}$                    & 1A, 2A          & 532           & 404       \\
    F$_{<:}$, records, patterns & 1B, 2B          & 715           & 818       \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}


The point of comparison is the substitution reasoning a de~Bruijn development
normally needs to do. For a fair comparison we use the Rocq solutions by the
\textsc{Autosubst} authors, where the $σ$-calculus laws are applied by the
\texttt{asimpl} tactic.

The $η$-laws, the ones we cannot register, are never needed. This is because
lifting is a primitive operation rather than one defined in terms of the
others.

Only one proof obligation for maps remains propositional. It comes up in Lemma~3.19
of POPLmark Reloaded, the extensionality lemma for strong normalization. The
$β$-step there produces $b\,\ag{[}\,\agc{`}\,x\,\ag{]₀}$, which has to meet an
anti-renaming lemma stated for renamings, and that requires
\[
  t\,\ag{[}\,\agc{`}\,x\,\ag{]₀} \;=\; t\,\ag{[}\,x \agop{∙ᴿ} \ag{idᴿ}\,\ag{]ᴿ},
\]
Its two sides are distinct normal forms in our rewrite system. The left one
unfolds to $t\,\ag{[}\,(\agc{`}\,x) \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{idˢ}\,\ag{]ˢ}$, an extension,
which $\mathsf{coincidence}$ cannot match. The rule that would bridge them is
$(\agc{`}\,x) \agop{∙ˢ} \ag{⟨}\,ξ\,\ag{⟩} \to \ag{⟨}\,x \agop{∙ᴿ} ξ\,\ag{⟩}$,
oriented $\mathsf{S}\to\mathsf{R}$ like the rest of the coercion group.
Registering it leaves non-joinable pairs that close only if composition at a
variable folds, against the push orientation of \cref{obs:fold-push}.

The same fact lies outside what \texttt{asimpl} normalizes in
\textsc{Autosubst}. There it is supplied by two separate tactics,
\texttt{substify} and \texttt{renamify}, that provide strategies to move a map
from one mode to the other.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:rwk}
Three lines of work meet here. Solutions to the POPLmark
Challenge~\cite{poplmark2005} fix the benchmark our evaluation is measured
against, generic and generated syntax libraries are what we would replace, and
a small recent literature attacks the same tension between provability and
definitional behaviour from the semantic side.

\emph{POPLmark solutions.} Fifteen solutions to the Challenge have been
published. Of those, four ever attempted the records extension, three the
pattern-matching extension, and four Part~3. Berghofer's Isabelle/HOL de~Bruijn
development is the only one covering Parts~1, 2 and~3 together. We cover 1A,
1B, 2A and~2B, and prove the congruence-rule presentation of reduction
equivalent to the challenge's evaluation contexts, for pure F$_{<:}$ and for
F$_{<:}$ with records. On POPLmark Reloaded we join
Allais's generic-syntax Agda solution, Beluga and the two Rocq
\textsc{Autosubst} solutions as a complete STLC and STLC$+$ solution. Allais
additionally proves completeness of the inductive characterization of strong
normalization, which we do not. The nearest neighbours in technique are Schäfer
and Stark's Rocq de~Bruijn solutions, by the authors of \textsc{Autosubst} and
of the Reloaded benchmark. Coverage is what makes the claim about the
substitution reasoning underneath it testable.

We have discussed \textsc{Autosubst} and related
research~\cite{10.1145/3293880.3294101, schafer2015autosubst,
  Stark:2020:Mechanising}, user-defined rewrite rules~\cite{10.1145/3434341,
  cockx:LIPIcs.TYPES.2019.2, leray_et_al:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.26} and opaque
definitions~\cite{gratzer2022controlling} throughout this work.

In recent work on local rewrite rules~\cite{10.1145/3776704}, the authors also
rewrite $σ$-calculus rules with support for first-class renamings in Rocq. They
too model renamings and substitutions as functions, but cannot rewrite all laws
against that model, because with local rewrite rules the instantiation of the
equations must hold definitionally rather than propositionally to maintain
consistency. Rocq also has global rewrite
rules~\cite{leray_et_al:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.26}, and we expect our approach to
translate. Rocq, however, cannot instantiate rewrite rules with proofs, so the
consistency guarantee would be lost.

Kaposi and Pujet obtain a definitional substitution calculus by a semantic
route~\cite{10.1145/3747535}, presenting the initial category with families so
that substitutions are metatheoretical functions inside strict presheaves over
the syntax of a dependent type theory. Composition of substitutions is then
composition of functions, so the laws hold by conversion and most of the
transports that an indexed-inductive presentation forces disappear. Their
strictified syntax has the induction principle of the initial model, which a
shallow embedding of the same syntax does not. In their formalization, ten of
the eleven equations of the strictified category with families hold by
reflexivity. The eleventh is surjective pairing for substitutions. We reach
that same law from the syntactic side, where inductive variables destroy its
left-hand side. Neither development makes surjective pairing definitional. They
derive lifting as $(γ \circ p, q)$ instead of taking it as primitive, so
$\ag{idˢ} \agop{↑ˢ} s = \ag{idˢ}$ fails for them, for the reason
\cref{sec:pre-sig} gives. We keep lifting primitive and keep that law. They
also obtain the law for the empty context, which we cannot register, because
the statement that any two substitutions out of the empty scope agree has no
left-hand side to match. Their strictness comes from the presentation rather
than from rewriting, but rewrite rules still carry their encoding of
observational type theory and of the quotient inductive-inductive syntax, and
those rules are postulated rather than proved. We prove every rule before
registering it, and we establish confluence and termination for the result.

Chen, Nordvall Forsberg and Tsai~\cite{10.1145/3779031.3779090} give an
intrinsic representation of type theory in Cubical Agda as quotient
inductive-inductive-recursive types, and obtain a syntax that needs no
transports, no postulates and no custom rewrite rules. Where they replace the
rewrite rules by a quotient, we keep the plain inductive type and take on a
confluence obligation instead.

Generators for binder boilerplate predate and accompany \textsc{Autosubst}.
Needle \& Knot~\cite{10.1007/978-3-662-49498-1_17} generates the boilerplate
for complex binding forms, including the relational lemmas that
\textsc{Autosubst} leaves out, but does not aim at an equational theory of
substitution and so has no equations to turn into rules. Strongly typed term
representations in Rocq~\cite{stronglytyped} are the ancestor of the scoped
syntax we use.

Another line of research concerns generic programming
approaches~\cite{allais2021typescopesafeuniverse, 10.1145/3018610.3018613} that
build syntax from general building blocks supporting sums, products and
binders, and thereby abstract over renamings and substitutions. The approach
carries over to a scoped, multi-sorted
setting~\cite{saffrich:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.32}. We believe generic programming and
rewriting the $σ$-calculus are orthogonal. Applying our rule set to the
universe of syntaxes with binding would remove the need for external code
generation altogether.

The \textsc{Abstract Binding Trees} library for Agda shares a goal with
\textsc{Autosubst} and is used, for instance, in Siek's
work~\cite{siek2021parameterized}. It builds an object language from a generic
%%CHANGED (related work): one clause trimmed, for the page budget.
syntax, but supports only extrinsic scoping and single-sorted syntax, and
focuses on generic theorems about substitution. Its experimental support for
rewriting with $σ$-calculus laws misses rules and does not pass the confluence
check.

Wadler experiments with explicit substitutions and implements weakening as a
constructor, thereby avoiding renamings~\cite{Wadler_2024}. He rewrites some of
the $σ$-calculus laws but does not pass the confluence checker.
\Cref{sec:introduction} discussed what that representation entails.

Wadler and colleagues also investigate a uniform treatment of renamings and
substitutions in a simply typed setting~\cite{altenkirch2025substitution},
originating in work by McBride~\cite{ren-sub}. It unifies repeated similar
lemmas, merging all four compositionality lemmas into one, by hiding the
structural dependence of renamings and substitutions in a uniform,
\AgdaDatatype{Mode}-indexed vector, and lets the termination checker see that
dependence where it matters. Saffrich~\cite{saffrich:LIPIcs.ITP.2024.32}
instead hides it behind instance resolution, in a scoped setting where the
uniform structure is again a function, and adds reflection-based code
generation for the syntax-dependent lemmas, which could be adopted here in
place of our external generator.

Adapting our approach to a setting where renamings and substitutions are
treated uniformly is a logical next step, but the equational theory would have
to be extended in non-obvious ways to support uniform proofs over both. It
would also require a two-level rewrite system, one on the type level,
normalizing expressions in the lattice of \AgdaDatatype{Mode}s generated by
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{V} $\sqsubseteq$
\AgdaInductiveConstructor{T}~\cite{altenkirch2025substitution}, and one for the
adapted $σ$-calculus. In our experiments the interaction of the two layers made
confluence hard to preserve.

\section{Future Work}\label{sec:future}

\subsection*{Opaque Definitions for Rewriting}
The construction suggests a general recipe for integrating normalization for
suitable algebraic structures into a proof assistant: define operations for the
structure; establish a confluent rewrite system over them; instantiate the
structure with an underlying model and prove the laws inside
\AgdaKeyword{opaque} blocks; and, with the model hidden, \AgdaKeyword{REWRITE}
the equations.

Classical sets are one candidate. Reasoning about them in Agda usually means
lists up to permutation together with a uniqueness predicate, which is tedious
and generates a great deal of uninteresting code. Their characteristic function
supplies the model the recipe asks for. Sets become \ESetProp\, for some
$\mathcal{A}$ : \AgdaDatatype{Set} \AgdaBound{ℓ}, the operations become opaque
functions \ESetOps{} and laws such as \ESetLawEx{} become provable and
registrable. The boolean ring interpretation~\cite{HSIANG1985255} is a natural
starting point for the rule set, but it needs rewriting modulo associativity
and commutativity. Dedukti~\cite{assaf2023deduktilogicalframeworkbased}
supports this and Agda does not, which is the missing ingredient.

\subsection*{Implications for Intrinsically Typed Syntax}\label{sec:future-int}
The construction applies as it stands to intrinsically typed syntax whose type
index lives in \AgdaDatatype{Set}. Richer syntax, where a substitution appears
in the type index, is out of its reach for now, and that is where the
phenomenon known as transport hell is worst. The transfer lemma
(\AgdaFunction{subst} in Agda) then has to carry every step of every proof.
Intrinsically typed System~F has been formalized in Agda before, executably and
in full~\cite{10.1007/978-3-030-33636-3_10}, and a binary logical relation over
it~\cite{10.1145/3678000.3678201} is a direct illustration. Type-in-type and
expression-in-expression reasoning stays easy, but type-in-expression
substitution also affects the index of the expression substituted into, and the
authors need thousands of lines for the resulting transports. Rewriting the
$σ$-calculus rules on the syntax of the typing index should remove them, and
should generalize to other intrinsically typed formalizations.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:con}

We have turned the normalization procedure of the $σ$-calculus into
definitional equalities in Agda. The operations must compute, so that the laws
are provable, and stay inert, so that the rules match. Opaque definitions give
both.

The theory we register is $σ_\Uparrow$ instantiated once per mode, with the
rules that first-class renamings force between the two. Which laws survive is
forced, because native inductive variables and surjective pairing cannot
coexist in one rewrite system. Agda certifies local confluence and AProVE
termination, a generator emits it from a syntax description, and the POPLmark
challenges exercise it at scale.

